{"name":"Gentry Grey","hex":"#bdae9e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gentry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Birth; condition; rank by birth.","Courtesy; civility; complaisance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gentry"},{"term":"grey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray.","Synonym of coloured (pertaining to the mixed race of black and white)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gentry","blocks":[{"text":"Gentry are \"well-born, genteel and well-bred people\" of high social class, especially in the past. Gentry, in its widest connotation, refers to people of good social position connected to landed estates, upper levels of the clergy, or long established \"gentle\" families of noble descent, some of whom in some cases never obtained the official right to bear a coat of arms. The gentry largely consisted of landowners who were supported entirely through rental income or at least had a country estate; some were gentleman farmers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Cleric%2C_Knight%2C_and_Workman_representing_the_Three_Classes.jpg/330px-Cleric%2C_Knight%2C_and_Workman_representing_the_Three_Classes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gentry","wikidata":"Q844586"},{"title":"Grey","blocks":[{"text":"Grey or gray is an intermediate color between black and white. It is a neutral or achromatic color, meaning that it has no chroma. It is the color of a rain or storm cloud, of ash, and of lead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg/330px-Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey","wikidata":"Q42519"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Three_kings_or_three_gods.jpg/500px-Three_kings_or_three_gods.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Three_kings_or_three_gods.jpg/1280px-Three_kings_or_three_gods.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Three_kings_or_three_gods.jpg/500px-Three_kings_or_three_gods.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This part of a 12th-century Swedish tapestry has been interpreted to show, from left to right, the one-eyed Odin, the hammer-wielding Thor and Freyr. This triad corresponds closely to the trifunctional division: Odin is the patron of priests and magicians, Thor of warriors, and Freyr of fertility and farming.[6]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThree_kings_or_three_gods.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Europe_1000.jpg/500px-Europe_1000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Europe_1000.jpg/1280px-Europe_1000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Europe_1000.jpg/500px-Europe_1000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Europe and the Byzantine Empire 1000 AD","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEurope_1000.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Bartholom%C3%A4us_Bruyn_-_The_Three_Estates_of_Christendom.jpg/500px-Bartholom%C3%A4us_Bruyn_-_The_Three_Estates_of_Christendom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia